          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-body">
        <div class="container article-shell">
          <div class="prose pillar-body">
            <p class="prose-lede">US publishing splits into five lanes, and where you can land a byline depends on which lane you aim at, not on how big your pitch list is.</p>

            <p>The national papers and the wire services do not run guest posts. The digital magazines and business titles do. The opinion sites want arguments. The metro dailies want a local hook. The small-business resources are the most open of all. The list of 100 is below, grouped roughly by lane.</p>

            <h2 id="tier">Pick your tier first</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Lane</th><th>Examples</th><th>How you get in</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>National news &amp; wires</td><td>NYT, Washington Post, WSJ, AP, Reuters, CNN, NPR</td><td>Not guest posts. An op-ed submission, expert commentary on a live story, or exclusive data</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Business, finance &amp; trade</td><td>Forbes, Inc, Entrepreneur, Fast Company, Fortune, Adweek, Business Insider</td><td>Contributor networks and section pitches; original data, founder lessons, industry analysis</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Digital magazines &amp; culture</td><td>Vox, Slate, The Atlantic, Rolling Stone, Vice, The Cut, GQ</td><td>Freelance features and essays with a fresh angle and reported detail</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Opinion &amp; commentary</td><td>The Hill, National Review, The Nation, Reason, RealClearPolitics</td><td>Tight, sourced arguments to the op-ed desk; no promotional links</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Metro dailies &amp; small business</td><td>SFGate, Chron, NJ.com, AJC, Score.org, Small Business Trends</td><td>Local angles, regional data, practical how-tos for owners</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="metrics">What the metrics mean</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it is</th><th>How much it matters</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Domain Rating (DR)</td><td>Ahrefs' 0 to 100 score for a whole site's backlink profile</td><td>A useful filter, not a verdict</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Domain Authority (DA)</td><td>Moz's equivalent 0 to 100 score</td><td>Same idea as DR</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Page Authority (PA)</td><td>Moz's score for one specific page</td><td>Matters once you know which page your post will live on</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>URL Rating (UR)</td><td>Ahrefs' score for one specific URL</td><td>Same purpose as PA</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Monthly Traffic</td><td>Estimated organic visits per month</td><td>The most honest signal that a link will be seen</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Here is the deal:</strong> almost every site on this list is DR 80-plus, so DR barely separates them. What separates a good placement from a wasted one is whether the section your post lands in is read by the people you want as customers.</p></div>

            <h2 id="list">The 100 US sites</h2>
            <p>Domain Rating, Domain Authority, Page Authority, URL Rating and estimated monthly traffic. Every domain was checked live in September 2026. Metrics are third-party estimates and shift over time.</p>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>#</th><th>Website</th><th>DR</th><th>DA</th><th>PA</th><th>UR</th><th>Monthly traffic</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {SITES.map((s) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td>{s.n}</td>
                      <td>{s.site}</td>
                      <td>{s.dr}</td>
                      <td>{s.da}</td>
                      <td>{s.pa}</td>
                      <td>{s.ur}</td>
                      <td>{s.traffic}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="pitch">The pitch that works for US editors</h2>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Lead with the US angle.</strong> US data, a US customer story, or a US policy hook in the first two lines. Editors here pass on anything that reads as generic or offshore.</li>
              <li><strong>Pitch the section, not the site.</strong> "For your Small Business section" or "for the Money desk" shows you have read the publication and know where the piece fits.</li>
              <li><strong>Bring something they cannot get from a staffer.</strong> Proprietary data, a survey, first-hand operator experience, or an interview they would not otherwise land.</li>
              <li><strong>Match the format to the lane.</strong> Op-ed desks want a 700-word argument. Business titles want a structured how-to with takeaways. Metro dailies want a local news peg.</li>
              <li><strong>Send one clip</strong> in a similar style, and your bio with the single link you would want.</li>
              <li><strong>Disclose any client or brand relationship</strong> up front. US editors will find it, and it ends the relationship if you hid it.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>The test:</strong> would this editor run the piece if you had nothing to sell? If yes, pitch it. If it only exists to place a link, it goes in the bin.</p></div>

            <h2 id="mistakes">What not to do</h2>
            <ul class="check-list">
              <li>Pitch the New York Times or WSJ a guest post. They do not exist there; you burn the contact.</li>
              <li>Send the same pitch to a national magazine and a metro daily. The angle has to change.</li>
              <li>Put exact-match anchors ("best CRM software") on links back to your product or store.</li>
              <li>Submit AI-generated filler with no US sourcing or first-hand detail. US desks screen for it now.</li>
              <li>Ignore the disclosure rules on money, health and legal topics. One buried conflict and the piece is pulled.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> a handful of placements in sections your US buyers actually read will move rankings and revenue more than a long list of high-DR links nobody in your market sees. Use this list to find your handful.</p></div>
          </div>

          <aside class="article-toc">
            <h4>On this page</h4>
            <ul>
              {toc.map((item) => (
                <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.text}</a></li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div id="faq">
        <Faq items={faqs} />
      </div>

      <section class="section section-alt">
        <div class="container">
          <div class="cta-panel" style="text-align: center; max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto;">
            <h2>Want us to run the outreach?</h2>
            <p>
              We build the shortlist by lane and section, write the pitches and the pieces, and
              place them on US publications with real editorial standards. No link farms, no risk.
            </p>
            <div class="hero-actions" style="justify-content: center; margin-top: 0;">
              <a href="/services" class="button">View services</a>
              <a
                href={whatsappUrl("Hi SERP Mentor, I would like help with US guest posting and digital PR.")}
                class="button-secondary"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                <i class="fa-brands fa-whatsapp" aria-hidden="true"></i> WhatsApp
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

    <SiteFooter />
  </body>
</html>
